WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.580
- Thanks to Brilliant for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.390
Get 20% off your annual subscription

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.500
by using the link in the description.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.480
The coronavirus is now officially killed

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.820
more people than SARS, and
it shows no sign of stopping.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.260
And it's not clear that the US government

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.590
is actually prepared for this.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:15.700
The Department of Homeland Security

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.180
is falsely saying that the mortality rate

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.180
of COVID-19 is comparable
to that of the flu.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.483
- Worldwide I believe it's under 2%.

00:00:21.483 --> 00:00:24.290
- What's the mortality rate for influenza,

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.350
over the last say 10 years in America?

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:29.673
- It's also right around
that percentage as well.

00:00:29.673 --> 00:00:32.670
- Actually the flu is
20 times less deadly,

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.460
and recently Vice President Mike Pence

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.510
was put in charge of the
government's response.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.460
Worse, there were reports of entire cities

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.000
being quarantined in China
and across the globe.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.368
But that's not America, right?

00:00:43.368 --> 00:00:44.850
Have you ever had a nightmare

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.300
about being permanently
confined to your house?

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.240
Can you imagine one day waking
up to find police officers

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.160
and soldiers right
outside your front door,

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.190
refusing to let you leave?

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.017
That could never happen in America,

00:00:56.017 --> 00:00:58.174
in response to a pandemic.

00:00:58.174 --> 00:00:59.521
Could it?

00:00:59.521 --> 00:01:02.188
(trumpet music)

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.890
Hey Legal Eagles it's time
to think like a lawyer

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.530
because the world is facing a pandemic.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.130
This is gonna be a bit more speculative

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.950
than most of the normal
videos on this channel

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.550
because we're going to
explore whether the US

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.434
could legally quarantine
towns, cities and states,

00:01:17.434 --> 00:01:20.320
the way some other countries are.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.620
You might be surprised to learn

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:25.670
that there is both US precedent,
and law on this subject.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.150
Now as of the time of this video

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.570
there are at least 80,000 confirmed cases

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.010
of the coronavirus worldwide

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.260
and almost 3000 deaths
attributable to the virus,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.420
and the numbers are steadily climbing.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.570
The question is how do you
stop the spread of a disease

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.830
or a virus that is asymptomatic?

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.580
This is the problem facing
China and the rest of the world.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.010
Now coronavirus is the name
for a large family of viruses

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.420
that can cause mild or severe illnesses

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.850
in humans and animals.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.090
There are seven different
strains of coronavirus

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:56.510
known to infect humans, and
before this most recent outbreak

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.580
only two of the coronaviruses were known

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.020
to cause serious illness.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.430
You might recognize them MERS and SARS.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.810
So what the heck is
this newest coronavirus?

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.200
Well officially it's called 2019-nCoV,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.450
and it hasn't been extensively studied.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.240
Doctors don't know where it came from,

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.640
most coronaviruses are
transmitted to humans

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.040
through an intermediate host.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.760
With SARS, the host was palm civets.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.610
For MERS, the host was
identified as camels.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:24.690
The host for the 2019 coronavirus

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:25.790
has not yet been identified,

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.010
but according to researchers
at Johns Hopkins,

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.570
it may have come from bats.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.250
Doctors are still working to understand

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.630
the 2019 coronavirus disease symptoms,

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.070
course and severity.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.850
Traditional flu symptoms like runny nose,

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:38.930
sore throat and vomiting
have only been reported

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.100
by about 2% of infected patients.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.500
Common symptoms seem to
include shortness of breath,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.648
fever and cough.

00:02:44.648 --> 00:02:47.010
But the problem with the 2019 coronavirus

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.790
is that people can spread the virus

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:50.410
before they're even symptomatic.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.110
So far almost 80,000 cases of coronavirus

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.350
have been reported in China

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:57.070
with about 14% classified as severe.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.250
Now, the first known
patient developed symptoms

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.860
as early as December,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.250
although China alerted the
World Health Organization

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:04.830
by the end of 2019,

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.660
China didn't act according
to lockdown the city of Wuhan

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.220
until January 23.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.910
At first, every patient's blood test

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:12.370
had to be transmitted to Beijing,

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.400
which slowed diagnosis and analysis,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.740
and the hospitals quickly became

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.180
overwhelmed and overcrowded.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.400
It appears the Chinese government

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.720
didn't want people to panic,

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.890
but withholding information
is rarely a good idea

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:23.560
in situations like these.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.250
Chinese physician Li Wenliang
and his fellow doctors

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.677
raced to warn the public of
the new coronavirus in 2019,

00:03:28.677 --> 00:03:30.940
but the Chinese government
immediately threatened

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.909
Dr. Wenliang for spreading false rumors.

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:36.790
Dr. Wenliang has now
died from the very virus

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.240
he was working to contain.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.710
This situation has caused
some people to draw analogies

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.620
between the way that the
Chinese are responding

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:44.410
to the coronavirus outbreak,

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.170
to the way that the Soviet
Union responded to Chernobyl.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.410
When the Soviet nuclear reactor broke down

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.170
triggering explosions,
fires and radioactive smoke.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.770
The Soviet government denied
the severity of the accident,

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.650
was slow to release
information to the public

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:59.430
and generally screwed
up the entire situation

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.170
into a historic tragedy.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.800
So before we talk about
the legality of isolation

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.100
and quarantines in the United States,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.940
let's actually talk
about what that entails.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.620
Societies, traditionally
have dealt with pandemics

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.030
by isolating people so
that they can prevent

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.350
the spread of contagious
disease to other people.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.140
Isolation means separating people

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:19.350
who have a contagious disease
from people who do not.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.920
Quarantine takes the
isolation one step further.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.840
Quarantine separates out people
with a contagious disease

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.770
and restricts the movement of people

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:29.118
who have been exposed
to contagious diseases,

00:04:29.118 --> 00:04:31.040
to see if they get sick.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.660
In either case, the goal is to stop

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.920
person-to-person
transmission of a disease.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.650
So what gives the government the right

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:38.440
to quarantine in the first place?

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.700
Isolation and quarantine
are not just medical terms.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.380
The government has generally speaking,

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.610
what's called police power
to sometimes in an emergency

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.750
legally isolate and quarantine patients.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.620
Police power in this context
means that the government

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.131
has a right to enforce laws,

00:04:51.131 --> 00:04:53.880
protect public welfare health and safety.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.470
If we're talking about
the federal government

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:56.487
then many legal scholars believe

00:04:56.487 --> 00:04:59.540
that police power is implied
in the Commerce Clause

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:00.510
to the Constitution.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:03.240
And in fact, by the time that
the constitution was drafted,

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.510
the US had already used quarantine powers

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.830
to deal with several public health crises.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.630
In the modern era the
federal law governing

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.150
isolation and quarantine
is found in Section 361

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.880
of the Public Health Service Act.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.140
This law authorizes the
US Secretary of Health

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.250
and Human Services to take
measures to prevent the entry

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:22.480
and spread of communicable
diseases, into the United States

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:23.830
and between the states.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:26.100
In fact, as is currently on the books,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.573
federal isolation and quarantine
is currently authorized

00:05:28.573 --> 00:05:31.080
for a list of contagious diseases

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.570
including cholera, diphtheria,
infectious tuberculosis,

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:38.040
plague, smallpox, yellow fever,
viral hemorrhagic fevers,

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.087
severe acute respiratory syndromes,

00:05:40.087 --> 00:05:42.720
and flu that can cause a pandemic.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.240
In order to keep this list flexible,

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.170
the President has the
power to revise this list

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:47.540
through executive order.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:50.320
But if quarantine and
isolation is necessary,

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.330
how would the government
carry out that order?

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:53.700
Well, under federal law

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.680
the Centers for Disease
Control and Prevention or CDC,

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.570
has the right to issue an
isolation or a quarantine order.

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.450
State and tribal governments
may issue their own orders

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:04.800
and ask federal authorities
for help enforcing them.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.470
And if necessary, the US
Customs and Border Patrol

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.630
and US Coast Guard may be
asked to enforce those orders.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.400
So the question is, is the US government

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.570
using isolation and quarantine now?

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:17.010
Well, you may be pleased
or terrified to know

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:19.210
that the US has actually now issued

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:22.590
its first quarantine order in 50 years.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:24.520
Recently health officials have quarantined

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.300
the 195 Americans who
were evacuated from Wuhan.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:32.190
All 195 people must remain
at the March Air Reserve Base

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.210
in California during the
incubation period of 14 days.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:36.870
20 doctors and nurses at the base

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.160
will monitor the passengers.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.620
If any of them starts to get sick

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.440
they will be transferred to a hospital.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.270
And in fact patients in New
York, Los Angeles and Chicago

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.400
are being monitored
for signs of the virus.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.100
And the Trump administration
recently announced

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:50.600
they will be suspending the
entry of any foreign national

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.648
who has traveled to China
over the last two weeks,

00:06:52.648 --> 00:06:55.680
unless they are immediate
family of a US citizen.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.480
In addition, any US citizen
who has been in China's

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.303
Hubei province within the last 14 days

00:07:00.303 --> 00:07:03.250
of their return will be
subject to up to 14 days

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.780
of mandatory quarantine to ensure

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.520
that they are provided medical care

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:07.690
and healthcare screening.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.790
According to the DHS order,

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:11.700
air travelers without an nexus to China

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.840
may also be routed to one of 11 airports

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.040
if they discover mid-flight

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.310
that someone on board has been
to China in the last 14 days.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:20.180
This might be completely necessary

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:21.810
but the history of
isolation and quarantine

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:24.290
in the United States
raises a lot of questions.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:27.530
But this is America, damn
it, and if there's one thing

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:30.020
that Americans like to
do, it's to sue people.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.720
So the question is,
could you as an American

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.190
sue the government over
a quarantine order,

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:37.537
and if you did what kind of
rights do you have to challenge

00:07:37.537 --> 00:07:40.020
what happens to you during confinement?

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:41.550
Well the answer to that question,

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:43.970
depends on what kind of disease you have,

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:45.909
whether the government
is acting arbitrarily,

00:07:45.909 --> 00:07:47.890
and what era you live in.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.330
So let's talk about the legal history

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:51.750
of isolation and quarantine
in the United States.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.700
The first era of quarantine
law runs from the colonial days

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:57.750
to the late 19th century, at
that time infectious diseases

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:00.230
were a deadly serious
threat to public health,

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:01.790
so it's probably not surprising

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:03.660
that there are a few cases challenging

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.340
the government's broad
authority to detain and restrain

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.620
individuals who were infected

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.600
or exposed to communicable diseases.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.560
In 1793, 5000 people died in Philadelphia

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.260
from yellow fever alone.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.200
Philadelphia was the
nation's capital at the time

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.650
and federal officials
evacuated and left the city

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.270
to deal with the fallout.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:21.680
So many people died that the city

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:23.256
lost a 10th of its population

00:08:23.256 --> 00:08:25.530
and the city government
effectively collapsed.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.370
The quarantine order sent
patients to a hospital

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:29.190
in the country, but it
wasn't very effective

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:31.930
since yellow fever is
spread through mosquitoes.

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:34.540
The crisis ended finally
when the weather turned cold

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:36.170
and killed off the mosquitoes.

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:39.380
Similarly, the New York typhus
outbreak of 1892 started

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.650
when certain immigrants
arrived in Ellis Island.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.170
Many of the passengers were
sick with typhus fever.

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:45.560
By the time the illness developed

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.130
most immigrants had already
settled on the Lower East Side

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:49.100
in New York City.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.790
The government rounded up those immigrants

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.060
and quarantine them on
North Brother Island.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.960
The incident provoked racist
and anti-semitic hysteria

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:57.040
in the newspapers.

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.010
And then there's of course,

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:00.380
the famous eponymous Typhoid Mary,

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:02.870
the nickname for a cook, Mary Mallon.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:05.570
Mary was an Irish immigrant
who carried the bacteria

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:07.060
that causes typhus fever.

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:09.040
Mallon was actually immune to the disease

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:11.360
but that didn't stop the disease
from spreading to others.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:14.570
And by working as a cook,
Mary Mallon had spread

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:16.110
a form of the salmonella disease

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.570
to people all over the city.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:20.220
Police also sent her
to North Brother Island

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.680
to avoid communicating it to other people.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:24.890
So Typhoid Mary's term of confinement

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:26.630
was supposed to be three years

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:28.810
as long as she agreed
never to work as a cook,

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:31.320
however Mallon later
resumed her work as a cook

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.900
and started another outbreak in 1915.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.740
Authorities again tracked her down

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:39.900
and sent her back to the
island for 23 more years.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:41.620
During this period quarantine was seen

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.060
as the ultimate public good.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.140
The Supreme Court said
that involuntary quarantine

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.000
was constitutional in a case that involved

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.050
quarantine of passengers from
eastern and southern Europe

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:51.330
on the Britannia.

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:53.190
American and Louisiana authorities

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:54.960
stopped passengers from leaving the ship

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.130
despite no evidence that anyone
had a contagious disease.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:09:59.820
The court said that the government

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:01.980
could hold the passengers indefinitely

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:03.500
because immigrants were on board

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.720
and of course immigrants carry diseases.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:07.840
Not really the courts finest moment

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:09.590
of constitutional analysis.

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:12.010
During this period courts
reviewed a lot of quarantine cases

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:13.770
but mostly they just checked
to see if the government

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:15.380
was following its own rules.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:17.850
It helps that the diseases
that people were worried about

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.060
were all highly communicable,

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.560
like yellow fever, typhus and smallpox.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.560
Moreover, almost everyone living

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:24.735
in the 18th and 19th centuries

00:10:24.735 --> 00:10:27.770
had lost someone to communicable disease,

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.260
since there were few effective treatments.

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:33.640
If a city like Philadelphia
could lose 10% of its population

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.650
very quickly, then communicable diseases

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:38.154
were seen as threats analogous to war.

00:10:38.154 --> 00:10:40.980
Americans would have to
wait until the second era

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:42.910
of quarantine cases to
challenge the validity

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:45.290
of involuntary separation and quarantine.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:47.620
In 1900, California federal court

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:50.220
became the first appeals court
to reject a quarantine law

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:51.330
on racial grounds.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.910
The city of San Francisco
issued an order requiring

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.780
all Chinese residents to be vaccinated

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.060
against bubonic plague.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:59.400
The inoculations turned out to be tainted.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:01.020
But the city argued that bubonic plague

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.470
was a serious problem
and that Asians as a race

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:05.790
were more likely to carry that disease.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:07.720
The California Court struck down that law

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:09.690
because it violated the
Equal Protection Clause.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:11.880
It discriminated against Asian immigrants

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.010
and the city also couldn't
back up its claims

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.290
that the inoculation could stop the spread

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:18.140
of the diseases it was targeting.

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:19.420
Of course, that didn't stop

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:20.850
the San Francisco health officials

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.540
who tried it a second time.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:24.330
This time, claiming that nine people died

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.730
from bubonic plague and that
the only way to contain it

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:30.200
was by quarantining people who
lived in Asian neighborhoods.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:32.390
At the same time, the city gave exemptions

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:33.870
to people who were not Asian

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:35.780
but lived in those neighborhoods.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:37.910
Appropriately the angry
federal district court

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:39.880
struck down this quarantine law as well.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:41.170
The key takeaway from these cases

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:42.690
is that if the government is reacting

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.870
to communicable diseases, the
threat had better be real.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:47.840
The government's action
can't just be an excuse

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:48.770
for discrimination.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:49.880
In the 20th century though

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:51.430
the government still found ways to take

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.740
its quarantine and isolation
powers in new directions.

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:56.210
The first target was prostitution.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:57.520
The idea was that prostitutes

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:59.660
spread the sexually transmitted diseases

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:01.520
and should be isolated and quarantined.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.570
The medical examinations
for venereal diseases

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:06.390
were more invasive of personal privacy.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:08.160
Police took advantage
of this quarantine power

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.120
and arrested and detained prostitutes

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.600
for reasons unrelated to
communicable diseases.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.220
And of course, there was stigma

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:15.600
towards anyone who was quarantined

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:17.050
for having a venereal disease.

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:20.060
This was a monumental shift
in how public health officials

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:22.900
conceptualize power to
stop contagious diseases.

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:24.820
But this new relationship
between public health

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.250
and criminal law forced courts
to give personal freedom,

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:28.450
a more serious look.

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:30.470
The California Courts were
the first in the country

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:32.610
to establish what the
government needed to prove

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:35.380
to detain someone who had
a communicable disease.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:37.430
At a bare minimum the
government had to establish

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:40.460
some reasonable ground to
believe that the targeted person

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:41.970
had been exposed or infected

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:43.410
with a contagious disease.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:44.413
Of course at the same time,

00:12:44.413 --> 00:12:47.970
people were facing real
risks from pandemics.

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:50.700
The influenza pandemic of 1917 and 1919,

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.040
killed around 50 million people.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.040
It resulted in mass quarantine orders

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:56.680
in bands of public gatherings,

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.880
and unfortunately the
isolation and quarantines

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:01.240
did little to stop the spread of disease.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.320
Influenza was highly contagious with

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:05.010
people becoming infected after exposure

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:06.270
of just a few minutes.

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:08.600
But given the danger
and the high death rate,

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:09.850
no serious legal challenges

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:11.170
seem to have been
brought during this time.

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:13.920
People were really just
trying to survive a world war

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:16.000
and global pandemic at the same time.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.580
So the question is can you challenge

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:19.958
a quarantine order
today and could you stop

00:13:19.958 --> 00:13:22.210
a quarantine in the United States?

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:24.040
Certainly modern medicine has progressed

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:26.050
a long way since 1917.

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:27.260
Well the answer is probably yes,

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:29.400
you can challenge it depending
on the circumstances.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:30.233
The Constitution says,

00:13:30.233 --> 00:13:31.927
"The privilege of the
writ of habeas corpus

00:13:31.927 --> 00:13:34.587
"shall not be suspended, unless
when in cases of rebellion

00:13:34.587 --> 00:13:37.470
"or invasion the public
safety may require it."

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.760
Generally this means that
a person has the right

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:42.480
to petition the court if
they are illegally confined.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.420
This procedure is used most often

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:45.640
to challenge a criminal conviction,

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:47.860
but it also applies to quarantine cases.

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:50.140
That being said, it's not often easy

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:51.630
to win a habeas case.

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:54.470
In 2014, Thomas Eric Duncan,
the first man in America

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:56.370
to be diagnosed with Ebola died

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.240
after Dallas hospital failed to recognize

00:13:58.240 --> 00:13:59.790
the signs of the disease.

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:02.180
They sent him home with
a sinusitis diagnosis

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:04.030
and his fiance Louise Troh,

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:05.420
experienced the nightmare scenario

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:07.180
I discussed at the beginning of the video.

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:10.060
She woke up one day to a
court order and armed guards

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:11.890
posted outside of her apartment.

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:14.330
Troh and her family were
confined for 14 days

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:15.769
and then sent into a Catholic Cottage

00:14:15.769 --> 00:14:18.360
for the remainder of a 21 day order.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:20.930
After Duncan's death, Troh's
apartment was fumigated

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:22.873
and many of her
possessions were destroyed.

00:14:22.873 --> 00:14:25.630
Troh was left homeless
after several landlords

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:27.640
denied her rental applications.

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.960
Troh didn't actually
challenge the order in court

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:32.500
but her case illustrates how
restrictive of personal freedom

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:34.130
quarantine and isolation can be,

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:36.000
limiting a person's
ability to travel, work

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:37.630
and to find a place to live.

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:40.380
And it's very difficult to
overturn one of these orders,

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:41.390
you have to show that the government

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:43.490
is acting in an arbitrary
and capricious manner.

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:45.390
The Florida Supreme Court
set the state standard

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:46.796
for reasonableness of a
state quarantine order

00:14:46.796 --> 00:14:49.800
in a 1956 case called Moore versus Draper.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:52.210
There the plaintiff had
challenged his quarantine

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:53.340
for tuberculosis.

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.117
The Florida court said that quote,

00:14:55.117 --> 00:14:57.097
"The constitutional
guarantees of life, liberty,

00:14:57.097 --> 00:14:59.217
"and property of which a
person cannot be deprived

00:14:59.217 --> 00:15:00.827
"without due process of law,

00:15:00.827 --> 00:15:03.227
"do not limit the exercise
of the police power

00:15:03.227 --> 00:15:05.337
"of the state to preserve
the public health,

00:15:05.337 --> 00:15:08.187
"so long as that power is
reasonably and fairly exercised

00:15:08.187 --> 00:15:09.490
"and not abused."

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:11.220
Although federal courts are not bound

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:12.995
by the Florida Supreme Court's decision,

00:15:12.995 --> 00:15:15.190
its statement of the law is regarded

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:16.800
as the general standard for determining

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:19.160
whether a person's due
process rights were violated.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:20.390
The decision gives great weight

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:22.280
to the government's determination
that the public health,

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:23.870
really is at stake.

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:26.180
And to bring it back
to the current pandemic

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:28.130
that we're faced with,
in our current scenario,

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:30.430
this wouldn't be a hard standard
for the government to meet.

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:33.000
Coronavirus 2019 is still spreading.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.550
People are still dying and researchers

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:36.550
don't have a firm handle
on the disease yet.

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:37.690
Though there are other issues

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:39.597
about how far authorities
can push their quarantine

00:15:39.597 --> 00:15:41.090
and isolation orders,

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:43.250
generally a person in a
civil confinement context

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:44.690
like isolation or quarantine

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:46.810
should be free from physical restraint.

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:49.580
But if you were subject to
isolation showing no symptoms

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:51.790
of infection and you tried to leave,

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:53.710
could authorities actually restrain you?

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:55.220
According to the Supreme Court,

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:56.660
the answer is probably yes,

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:58.640
if you are quote, "a dangerous patient."

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:00.530
Again, it's pretty easy
for the state to prove

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:03.080
that a person is dangerous
if they are contagious.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:04.860
Authorities only needs
to show that the reason

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:07.440
for the restraint and
confinement is not arbitrary,

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:08.960
but that's not to say
that there aren't cases

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:10.810
where the government
has acted unreasonably.

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.470
For example in 2007,

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:15.110
the ACLU challenged a
dangerous patient determination

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:16.660
on behalf of Robert Daniels,

00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:18.209
a quarantined tuberculosis patient

00:16:18.209 --> 00:16:20.850
who emigrated from Russia to Arizona.

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:23.820
Daniels had been diagnosed
with dangerous TB

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:25.700
and when Daniels defined a court order

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:28.200
requiring him to wear
a face mask he refused,

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:29.860
leading to his arrest and confinement

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:31.490
in the Maricopa County Jail.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:33.770
Maricopa County Sheriff Joe Arpaio,

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:34.931
you might remember that guy,

00:16:34.931 --> 00:16:38.380
kept Daniels in solitary
confinement for nearly a year.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:41.120
After the ACLU lawsuit the
county transferred Daniels

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:42.800
to national Jewish hospital

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:43.800
where physicians determined

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:47.150
that he actually didn't have
dangerous and contagious TB.

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:48.990
After which Daniel's returned to Russia,

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.040
Arpaio was later convicted
of criminal contempt

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:53.030
for ignoring a court order
in a separate matter.

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:54.960
Donald Trump then pardoned Arpaio

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.600
and they all live happily ever after.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:59.650
- I stand by my partner Sheriff Joe--

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:00.930
- And as for whether the US government

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:04.450
could affect a nation
or statewide quarantine,

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:06.280
well, the government has wide latitude

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:07.750
to isolate and quarantine anyone

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:09.570
who has been in the area of Wuhan

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:11.060
over the last couple of months.

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:11.990
But for better or worse,

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:13.830
generally the isolation
and quarantine orders

00:17:13.830 --> 00:17:16.170
can't last longer than
the incubation period

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:17.700
of the targeted disease.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.010
The CDC currently estimates
that the incubation period

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:23.160
is about 14 days for the 2019 coronavirus

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:25.880
based on previous iterations, like MERS.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:28.410
Of course, the reason
governments resort to quarantine

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:31.130
is because the virus tends
to spread exponentially.

